---
// Page shell for /tools/diversification: intro + the DiversificationModule
// island + sources and next-tool link.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DiversificationModule from "../../components/DiversificationModule.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import RegionNote from "../../components/RegionNote.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Diversification"
  description="Diversification from first principles: how out-of-phase ups and downs cancel to lower risk. Start with the pure idea as interfering waves, then add real, noisy returns and watch the free lunch shrink but not vanish."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Diversification: the only free lunch</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Mixing assets that don't move in lockstep lowers a portfolio's risk without
      lowering its expected return: the closest thing investing has to a free
      lunch. Meet it in three views: first the pure idea, where out-of-phase waves
      cancel; then the messy reality, where noise means cancellation is only
      partial and every so often everything falls together; and finally its
      opposite, where a "500-stock" index turns out to ride on a handful of giants.
    </p>
    <div
      class="intro-formula"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Portfolio variance equals w-one squared sigma-one squared plus w-two squared sigma-two squared plus two times w-one w-two rho sigma-one sigma-two"
    >
      <span class="intro-formula-eq">σ<sub>p</sub><sup>2</sup> = w<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>σ<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + w<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>σ<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> + 2w<sub>1</sub>w<sub>2</sub> ρ σ<sub>1</sub>σ<sub>2</sub></span>
      <span class="intro-formula-key">
        <b>σ<sub>p</sub></b> portfolio risk · <b>w</b> each asset's weight ·
        <b>σ</b> each asset's own risk · <b>ρ</b> their correlation — the term that
        <em>shrinks</em> risk when they don't move together.
      </span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <DiversificationModule client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <RegionNote kind="data">
      The <strong>How top-heavy is the market?</strong> tab uses US data — the
      S&amp;P 500's own constituents (CRSP). The lesson is universal: any
      cap-weighted index, in any country, can be quietly dominated by a few
      giants. The specific names are American.
    </RegionNote>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Waves: the pure idea</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Each asset is a wave.</strong> Taller = more volatile; its
          <strong>correlation</strong> sets its phase. At +1 the waves line up; at
          −1 they're mirror images.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The portfolio is their sum.</strong> The dashed lines show how
          big the volatility would be if risk simply stacked; the gap down to the solid
          wave is the risk that cancelled away. Slide correlation toward −1 and the
          portfolio flattens toward a straight line: near-perfect cancellation.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Messy reality</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing cancels perfectly.</strong> With real, noisy returns the
          theoretical benefit and what a given run actually delivers rarely match,
          and there's no truly risk-free blend of risky assets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Assets sink together.</strong> The higher the correlation, the
          more often every holding is down at once: exactly when diversification
          helps least. That's why correlations, not just volatilities, drive
          portfolio risk.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Concentration: diversification in reverse</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>"500 stocks" can mean a handful.</strong> A cap-weighted index
          hands the biggest companies the biggest share, so when a few giants swell,
          the top 10 names can make up a third or more of the whole. The
          <strong>effective number of stocks</strong> (1 ÷ the sum of squared
          weights) shows how few names you're really diversified across.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Concentration is a bet.</strong> Equal-weighting every stock and
          cap-weighting the same 500 give very different rides: cap weight wins when
          the giants lead, and lags when they don't. Owning the index is convenient,
          but it isn't the same as owning 500 things equally.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is the core idea behind <strong>Modern Portfolio Theory</strong>,
        introduced by Harry Markowitz in 1952: because correlations, not just
        volatilities, drive a portfolio's risk, the <em>mix</em> of assets can be
        engineered for a better risk-and-return trade-off. Formalized, that search
        is called <strong>mean-variance optimization</strong>: using expected
        returns, variances, and correlations to find the least-risk portfolio for a
        given expected return. Both views here are simplified, and real returns have
        fat tails the model can't capture. To see that optimization drawn as the
        efficient frontier, with a full range of outcomes, continue to
        <a href="/tools/portfolio">Portfolio, Allocation &amp; Bonds</a>.
        Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="diversification" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/diversification" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure-wide);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>
